crime.gov.in).","Notify the organization being impersonated (e.g., your bank or service provider).","Monitor your financial statements for any unauthorized transactions for the next 30 days."]},"nextSteps":["Immediately contact your bank's official customer care number (from the back of your ATM card or bank website), report the fraud, and request to block your ATM card and enable a hold on your account and any linked services.","Visit your bank branch in person with identity proof, explain the incident, and submit a written complaint requesting reversal/freezing of fraudulent transactions and a detailed account statement for the relevant period.","File a formal cyber crime complaint on the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (https://www.cybercrime.gov.in/) or by calling the cyber helpline (1930), providing exact transaction amounts, times, and any available SMS screenshots.","Preserve all evidence: do not delete SMS messages, call logs, or bank notifications related to the incident; take clear screenshots and keep your phone safely available for investigation.","Note down and share with police the approximate time of the call, the name of the bank involved, and, if possible, retrieve the caller’s number from your phone’s call history.","Request the investigating officer to send urgent communication to the bank(s)/wallet(s) where the money was transferred to attempt recovery or freezing of funds.","Do not share ATM card numbers, PIN, CVV, or OTP over phone, SMS, or email in future, even if the caller claims to be from the bank or government; banks never ask for OTP or full card details over phone.","Ask the bank to issue a new ATM/debit card and change your mobile banking/Internet banking passwords and PINs, if any.","Follow up regularly with both the bank and the cyber crime police station and keep copies of all complaints and acknowledgments for your records."],"description":"To,\nThe Officer-in-Charge,\nCyber Crime Police Station.\n\nSubject: Complaint regarding Financial Fraud through Fake Bank Phone Call.\n\nI, Ramesh, wish to report a fraudulent incident involving unauthorized withdrawal of money from my bank account by an unknown person posing as a bank representative.\n\n1. Incident Details:\n   - Date & Time: Yesterday morning around 9:00–10:00 AM (exact date not recalled; information not available at the time of reporting).\n   - Mode of Communication: Phone call (voice call).\n   - Suspect Details: Unknown male caller claiming to be from my bank. He stated that my ATM card would be blocked due to non-updation of KYC. The mobile number used by the caller is not mentioned here as I do not have it readily available at the time of reporting.\n\n2. Chronological Description of the Incident:\n   I am a resident of Vijayawada and I use my bank account to receive my pension. Yesterday morning, at approximately 9:00–10:00 AM, I received a phone call from an unknown person who claimed that he was calling from my bank. He informed me that my ATM card would be blocked because my KYC was not updated. I became worried since my pension is credited to this account.\n\n   The caller told me that I did not need to visit the bank and that he would help me update the KYC over the phone. Trusting that he was a genuine bank employee, I provided my ATM card number and the One-Time Password (OTP) received on my registered mobile number as instructed by him.\n\n   After a few minutes, I started receiving SMS alerts from my bank indicating that multiple transactions were being carried out from my account. The messages showed that first an amount of Rs. 10,000, then Rs. 15,000, and then Rs. 20,000 had been debited from my account, totaling Rs. 45,000. Realizing that something was wrong, I immediately tried to call back on the same number, but the phone was switched off.\n\n   I informed my son about the incident, and after checking the messages and account status, he confirmed that a fraud had taken place. I did not physically hand over any cash to anyone. The entire fraud happened through the phone call and misuse of my ATM card details and OTP.\n\n3. Financial Loss Details:\n   - Amount Debited: Rs. 10,000 + Rs. 15,000 + Rs. 20,000.\n   - Total Financial Loss: Rs. 45,000 (Rupees Forty-Five Thousand only).\n   - Mode of Loss: Unauthorized online/ATM/card-not-present transactions after sharing ATM card number and OTP over a fraudulent phone call.\n\n4. Evidence List:\n   - Bank SMS alerts showing debit transactions of Rs. 10,000, Rs. 15,000, and Rs. 20,000 (available on my mobile phone).\n   - Call log on my mobile phone showing the incoming call from the fraudster (exact number can be retrieved from my phone records).\n   - Bank account statement reflecting the unauthorized transactions (can be obtained from the concerned bank).\n\n5. Request for Action:\n   I kindly request you to:\n   - Register my complaint as an FIR regarding this cyber-enabled financial fraud.\n   - Coordinate with the concerned bank to trace, block, and attempt to recover the fraudulently transferred amount of Rs. 45,000.\n   - Trace the mobile number and account/merchant/wallet details to which the money was transferred and identify the person(s) involved.\n   - Take appropriate legal action against the fraudster(s) under relevant sections of the law.\n   - Issue necessary directions to the bank to secure my account and prevent any further unauthorized transactions.\n\n   I am ready to provide my bank details, SMS screenshots, call logs, identity proof, and any other information required to assist in the investigation.\n\nYours faithfully,\nRamesh\nResident of Vijayawada","incidentType":"financial_fraud","modeOfAttack":"Phone Call","extractedDetails":{"date":"Information not available at the time of reporting (described as 'yesterday')","time":"Around 9:00–10:00 AM","data_shared":"ATM card number and mobile OTP","loss_amount":"45000","account_usage":"Pension money account","suspect_details":"Unknown male caller claiming to be from the complainant's bank and stating ATM card would be blocked due to non-updation of KYC","complainant_name":"Ramesh","transaction_breakup":[{"amount":"10000"},{"amount":"15000"},{"amount":"20000"}],"caller_number_status":"Number switched off when called back","complainant_location":"Vijayawada","mode_of_communication":"Phone call"},"suggestedCategory":"Online Banking & Card-Related Financial Fraud (Vishing/KYC Scam)"},"status":"draft","createdAt":"2026-05-13T03:00:40.602Z"},{"id":18,"userId":"53748919","title":"Money Deduction","rawDescription":"My name is Ramesh. I am from Vijayawada. Yesterday morning around 9 or 10 I got one phone call. That person told he is calling from bank and my ATM card will be blocked because KYC not updated. I got scared because I am using that account for my pension money.\n\nHe told me no need to come bank, he will help on phone only. He asked my ATM card number and mobile OTP. I told because I thought real bank person only. After some minutes I got messages that money is going from my account. First 10000, then 15000, then again 20000. Total 45000 rupees gone.\n\nI called that number again but switched off. I told my son and he checked and said fraud happened. I don’t know how this happened. I did not give money to anyone directly. Please help me get my money back and catch that person","incidentType":"PHISHING","structuredReport":{"impact":"Loss of Rs. 45,000 from bank account via unauthorized transactions following a fraudulent phone call in which ATM card details and OTP were obtained.","guidance":{"evidence":["Take screenshots of the phishing email or SMS, including the sender's details.","Do not delete the original message; it contains technical headers needed for investigation.","Note down the URL of the malicious website if you clicked a link."],"security":["Change passwords for your email and any affected online accounts immediately.","Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) on all sensitive accounts.","Run a full antivi